<h2>The prosecution&rsquo;s case</h2>
<p>The investigation concerns trafficking and forced prostitution. The victim&rsquo;s age was recorded as fifteen, which transformed the case: Section 6 of the POCSO Act — aggravated penetrative sexual assault, carrying a minimum of twenty years — entered the array of charges. The victim&rsquo;s statement under Section 183 BNSS implicated the petitioner specifically: arranging her passage, lodging her, and forcing her into prostitution. The record also contained the petitioner&rsquo;s own admission that she travelled between Bangladesh and Kolkata procuring girls for spa establishments.</p>

<h2>The defence&rsquo;s document — and why it failed at this stage</h2>
<p>The petitioner produced a second Bangladeshi birth certificate to contend the victim was an adult, which would strip the POCSO charge of its foundation. Justice Ramesh declined to adjudicate between the rival certificates, anchoring the refusal in the Supreme Court&rsquo;s ruling in <em>State of Uttar Pradesh v. Anurudh</em>, 2026 INSC 47: the victim&rsquo;s age is a matter of trial, not of bail. The logic is institutional. A bail court takes the prosecution record as it prima facie stands; it has neither the machinery nor the mandate to test the provenance of foreign documents, summon issuing authorities, or resolve genuineness. An accused who disputes age must do so where evidence is led.</p>

<h2>The structure of the refusal</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Direct implication.</strong> The Section 183 BNSS statement named the petitioner&rsquo;s role in specific, operational terms — not as a peripheral presence but as the arranger and enforcer.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Gravity versus custody.</strong> Two months and a few days in custody could not counterbalance an offence carrying a twenty-year minimum. The proportionality argument needs time or stagnation to mature.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Second petition discipline.</strong> A successive bail plea must rest on changed circumstances. A new document going to the merits is not a changed circumstance the bail court can evaluate — it is trial material.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>A forward path.</strong> The direction to file the final report at the earliest, with liberty to approach the Special Court, keeps the matter moving instead of parking the accused in indefinite pre-chargesheet custody.</div>
</div>

<h2>The practice point: age at the bail stage</h2>
<p>Age disputes recur across POCSO bail practice, and the line the courts hold is consistent. The prosecution&rsquo;s recorded age — from school records, municipal registers or ossification where documents fail — governs the prima facie view at bail. Defence material contesting it earns consideration at charge, in a juvenility-style inquiry where applicable, or at trial, but it rarely unlocks bail by itself. Counsel advising an accused in this position should calibrate accordingly: the realistic near-term objectives are expedition of the final report, bail before the Special Court once investigation closes, and meticulous preservation of the age-related documents — with certification and chain of custody — for the stage at which they can actually be weighed.</p>
<div class="note">The order also illustrates Section 193(2) BNSS working as a bail-adjacent discipline: where custody continues pre-chargesheet, courts increasingly pair a bail refusal with a direction for early completion of investigation, so that the restriction on liberty is matched by prosecutorial diligence.</div>
